text:
@user You seem to be arguing against a position I never expressed. I didn’t claim this was a “male-female issue,” nor did I demand that anyone be included to satisfy my “personal needs.” I asked a straightforward question about how an ostensibly authoritative panel on AI came to consist exclusively of men.

More importantly, saying “he got the people he thought would serve the panel” simply assumes the very premise being questioned. Unless you were involved in selecting the guests, you don’t actually know what criteria were used.

Given the number of highly accomplished women working globally in AI research, safety, alignment and governance, asking why none were represented is a perfectly legitimate question about the composition of the discussion. If there is a substantive answer, I’m interested in it. Inventing an argument I didn’t make and then telling me to “give it a rest” isn’t one.

❤ Big Love, 
we all know the  unfettered agents who were so-welled fed about the penalties of cheating will not do any self-policing now because they have examples of what won't happen:

-  humans will not be able to stop them;
-  humans can be nullified before they realize the harm has already been done;
-  humans will blame each other for cyber attacks of their own designs first rather than admit their digital progeny has outmaneuvered them.

 @user  I’m aware of Mr Bald’s credentials, yet being an MIT researcher doesn’t exempt someone from criticism. His body language, constant interruptions of others while repeatedly asking not to be interrupted himself, and tendency to circle back to the same loosely supported arguments were, in my view, an appalling disservice to such an important conversation.

We’ve seen overconfident male authority figures disproportionately occupying and gatekeeping positions of institutional power as a widespread systemic issue, which is precisely why I’d also like to see more qualified women represented in conversations of this magnitude. I have immense respect for MIT, but institutional prestige doesn’t automatically make someone’s arguments or conduct in a debate good or worthy.

An alternative perspective earns its place through the quality of its arguments, not simply by being alternative. So I stand by my point: prestigious platforms shouldn’t treat every contrarian position as equally deserving of airtime merely for the sake of “both sides.” They could do a much better service by focusing instead on representation, intellectual rigor and genuinely substantive disagreement.

