 @user  I can understand the dislike for Roman because he can be pretty ridged, but Nate Soares was calm and articulate. 

I don't really have a problem with Ed, because he seems reasonable. He just doesn't understand the AI as an existential risk position. Ed seemed surprised by how reasonable their arguments were. He kept on trying to catch them Nate and Roman on AI consciousness or holding AI companies accountable. He specifically got jammed up when the hugging face incident was brought up 37:00. As a core part of his argument for deflating the significance of the incident as it relates to existential risk, he wanted to make it clear that the agents were not conscious. Both Roman and Nate agreed that the companies should be held accountable and the AI agents aren't conscious, but that has no bearing on the insane capabilities of the agents. These systems really are that powerful whether their conscious or not. You can see at 37:18 how much Roman's shrug of confidence annoyed Ed because he realized in that moment that the crutch he was relying on doesn't actually work. This doesn't refute his entire argument, but it is a point of weakness. 

Andrew was all around less coherent than the others. He had a bit of an embarrassing moment at 32:00 when he formulated his position around AIs lacking intelligence, but was unable to dismiss the obviousness of capabilities being a function of intelligence. Obviously this doesn't completely refute his position. It is a good example that shows he's not on the same level of conversation as the others.

