 @user  You really ought to open your eyes. The people calling for a slowdown are precisely Dario Amodei, Sam Altman, and Elon Musk, along with their cheerleaders online. A couple of people did quit to make the whole thing look more serious, but literally just a couple. And there are also a few podcasters cashing in on the views this generates.

And yes, I’m a mathematician and a programmer, and I understand that, by and large, they’re talking nonsense. In many cases, they’re also simply lying about the facts. The technology isn’t remotely as impressive as they’re trying to make it out to be. And the theoretical debate about the dangers of AI hasn’t really moved beyond The Terminator.

If you think that someone saying “I know the chess champion will win, but I don’t know which piece they’ll win with” is just a broken record, and that my only argument is serious reasoning and the fact that I’m a specialist, then you genuinely don’t understand what a specialist is.

 @user  AI will speed up our advancement in a wide range of fields that will lead to increased productivity. Robotics is one of them, as is biochemistry, physics, etc. Companies are pouring billions of dollars  into AI because they expect a return on investment at some point. That return needs to be something concrete, and not cute ephemeral ideas. 

Even in the field of biochemistry, AI is leading to accelerating advancements, some of them revolutionary that would have taken humans decades longer to uncover without it. When we can't understand those solutions, we may unknowingly use AI ourselves to create drugs or modified viruses that operate in ways we do not understand. AI has already designed new drugs, including 1 in clinical phase 2 trials that shows promise to increase human longevity. Could these drugs turn out to be trojan horses that we built following the directions by AI?  

So robotics isn't required for AI to physically affect humanity.  Obviously we make AI to positively affect real life in order to make money. We have no guarantee that those effects will remain positive.

