 @user  to be absolutely fair you have to remember that human eyes have a blind spot where the optic nerve bundle exits the retina. These are filled in by your brain so it's unnoticeable, but those blind spots exist. Similarly a lot of what you visually perceive is interpretation by your brain, not necessarily exactly what's there. Perceptual bias, pattern recognition and attention etc. This can, and does, lead to driving accidents.

Self driving cars fail because the model doesn't know, or chooses incorrectly, when handling novel situations. They can't recognise the limitations of their perception, they don't have a history of unrelated experience to draw on to identify that a picture of a tunnel isn't a tunnel etc. They've never seen road runner. They can be backed up by a range of different sensor platforms though, albeit the underlying issues remain.

It's not because of the camera optics.

